// src/kernel/core/policy/thermal_policy.pdx — R31.M2-003 (#1096)
//
// The three-level cooling-intent map, and the pure computation that turns
// a thermal-zone level into one of {NONE, ACTIVE, CRITICAL}.
//
// =============================================================================
// §0  WHAT THIS MODULE IS AND WHAT IT IS NOT
// =============================================================================
//
// It is a TABLE from KIND_THERMAL_ZONE's six-level ladder to a three-value
// cooling INTENT enumeration, and two functions that read that table --
// one from a level, one from a live zone slot. That is all. There is no
// fan here, no shutdown path here, no channel here, and NO CALL that
// changes physical machine state. #1590 unwound three closed issues that
// tried to say otherwise, and `tests/kernel/cap/thermal_zone_synth.pdx`
// header §"What this witness asserts" records it in the sibling file.
//
// The name `thermal_policy` matches `design/roadmap/next-wave-softarch.md`
// §5 (R31.M2 -- `thermal_policy` server) and the module is what the
// eventual userspace server invokes when it holds a KIND_COOLING_DEVICE
// and can convert an intent into a fan write. It is NOT the server: the
// server is a ring-3 process that subscribes to thermal-zone events and
// dispatches through a cooling channel, neither of which exists yet.
// #1103 (KIND_COOLING_DEVICE), #1097 (AML _TMP / _CRT reader) and a
// consumer of `THERMAL_OP_ASSESS` are the three deferrals that keep this
// as a computation rather than a service.
//
// Writing it as an in-kernel table has two properties the eventual
// userspace host would not have. First, the table's authority is proved
// by the boot capability rather than by an authority the ring-3 process
// argues for -- the tunability question ("who may install cooling
// meanings") answers itself while the caps that would grant it do not
// exist. Second, the pure computation is testable at boot, so the
// arithmetic and the refusals of `map_install` are witnessed before any
// consumer arrives -- exactly the shape §6 of `kind_thermal_zone.pdx`
// asks a future consumer to have.
//
// =============================================================================
// §1  THE THREE INTENTS, AND WHY THREE
// =============================================================================
//
// The kind-side ladder has six levels: NOMINAL, ACTIVE1, ACTIVE2, ACTIVE3,
// PASSIVE, CRITICAL. The issue asks for a three-level algorithm, and the
// grouping is not arbitrary -- three is the number of distinguishable
// physical actions a cooling actuator this kernel can address:
//
//   INTENT_NONE      Do nothing. The zone is at NOMINAL and hysteresis has
//                    not put it there falsely.
//   INTENT_ACTIVE    Engage cooling. The zone is on the active ladder or
//                    on the passive step. The consumer decides WHICH
//                    cooling device from its own capability set; this
//                    module names the DECISION and not the actuator, in
//                    the same shape ec_event_shed_policy names admission
//                    without naming the ring.
//   INTENT_CRITICAL  A critical crossing has been confirmed on this zone
//                    (§3's two-agreeing-reads gate; see kind's §3). This
//                    is the intent a platform-shutdown path would consume.
//                    No shutdown path exists yet -- kind §6 records it --
//                    and this intent is the placeholder the consumer will
//                    read rather than re-derive.
//
// A finer partition would be a lie: three cooling levels grouped from
// three ACTIVE ladder steps is still three levels applied to a hardware
// that offers, on the T14 G4, ONE fan whose speed is a byte. Coarser
// than three would collapse CRITICAL into ACTIVE, which is the collapse
// the kind's §1 refuses: "confusing _CRT with _PSV in either direction
// is a shipped disaster."
//
// The 0 code is DELIBERATELY UNSET rather than NONE, mirroring
// EC_EVT_CLASS_NONE-vs-unmapped-byte in `core/acpi/ec_event.pdx`. The
// difference is what happens on an uninstalled level: ec_event lets an
// unmapped byte STILL ROUTE (NONE with class), because refusing delivery
// is the kernel censoring data. Here the answer is the opposite --
// INTENT_UNSET propagates as THERMAL_POLICY_INTENT_BAD -- because a
// caller that read INTENT_NONE for a CRITICAL level and did nothing
// would have been misled by silence into leaving a hot part on.
//
// =============================================================================
// §2  THE MAP IS INSTALLED, NOT COMPILED IN
// =============================================================================
//
// Same discipline as the query-byte meanings in `core/acpi/ec_event.pdx`
// §2 and the trip table in `core/cap/kind_thermal_zone.pdx` §2, and the
// same reason: the level-to-intent binding is a POLICY, and a compiled-in
// policy is the kernel imposing one -- which on a laptop where the
// operator has three thermal profiles is the kernel picking one for them.
//
// So the module holds a zero-initialised table, `_thermal_policy_map`,
// and every entry starts UNSET. The intended installer is either the
// ring-3 thermal-policy server (once it exists) or, for the boot witness,
// `thermal_policy_map_reset` which fills the six-level default at once.
// A caller that queries a level before its meaning is installed sees
// THERMAL_POLICY_INTENT_BAD and knows.
//
// MONOTONICITY, in the same shape ec_event_map_set holds it (§3 there):
// once a level acquires a non-UNSET intent, re-installing the SAME intent
// is idempotent OK, and re-installing a DIFFERENT one is refused
// (THERMAL_POLICY_MAP_ALREADY). A NOMINAL that means NONE at boot and
// ACTIVE an hour later would silently reinterpret the same reading -- the
// mirror of the "byte that means POWER at boot and HOTKEY an hour later"
// case ec_event's §3 refuses.
//
// Total re-tuning is `thermal_policy_map_reset` followed by six installs.
// A separate `_uninstall` call is deliberately absent for the reason the
// kind's `_set_crt` is: the monotone install and the wholesale reset are
// the two shapes worth having, and a per-entry clear is a way to make one
// live entry disappear without the reset that lets a reader see the
// transition.
//
// =============================================================================
// §3  A LATCHED CRITICAL CROSSING OVERRIDES THE LEVEL
// =============================================================================
//
// `thermal_policy_intent_of_slot` composes two reads. The first is
// `thermal_zone_crt_latched_of_slot`; if it answers 1, the intent is
// CRITICAL regardless of what the current level is. The reason is stated
// in the kind's §3 and again in the justification of
// `thermal_zone_row_latched`: the latch is the ROW'S MEMORY that a part
// went past its limit, and a consumer that re-derived the decision from
// the current level would erase that memory the moment temperature dipped
// under `_CRT`.
//
// Only after the latch is clear does the second read happen: the current
// level via `thermal_zone_level_of_slot`, then the table lookup. That
// order is what makes a latched zone that has cooled BELOW its ACTIVE
// thresholds still report CRITICAL -- exactly the case that would fool
// a naive lookup into reporting NONE for a still-dangerous part.
//
// =============================================================================
// §4  WHAT THIS MODULE STILL DOES NOT DO
// =============================================================================
//
// Stated plainly, in the manner of `core/cap/kind_thermal_zone.pdx` §6:
//
//   * NOTHING DRIVES A FAN. The consumer of INTENT_ACTIVE will be the
//     ring-3 process that holds a KIND_COOLING_DEVICE capability. #1103
//     is where that kind is introduced.
//   * NOTHING POWERS THE MACHINE OFF. INTENT_CRITICAL exists so that the
//     platform shutdown path, when it is written, reads the intent rather
//     than re-deriving it from a temperature. #1580 keeps EC access
//     unarbitrated against SMM, and the kind's §3 B9 is the only assertion
//     in the tree that says a single reading above _CRT does not authorise
//     that path.
//   * NOTHING SUBSCRIBES TO A ZONE. The full server is a ring-3 process
//     that subscribes to a thermal-notification channel and calls
//     `thermal_policy_intent_of_slot` on every wake. #1097 (AML _TMP)
//     is what makes a reading arrive to be assessed at all.
//   * NOTHING TUNES THE MAP FROM RING 3. `thermal_policy_map_install` is
//     a kernel function today; the syscall shim that exposes it will
//     arrive with the userspace server, and it will be gated by a
//     capability rather than by the boot authority the kernel witness
//     uses to reach it.
//
// The boot witness therefore asserts installation, monotonicity, table
// refusals and slot resolution. It asserts nothing about a fan.
//
// =============================================================================
// §5  ERROR CODES 0xFFFFFE20..0xFFFFFE2A
// =============================================================================
//
// The band is disjoint from every other in tree: the owner column owns
// 0xFFFFFE2B..2F, the thermal-zone kind owns 0xFFFFFE30..3F, and
// 0xFFFFFE20..2A was free. Disjointness is what lets a caller that threads
// a code up through the level lookup and the slot resolver still name the
// gate that refused it.
//
// =============================================================================

module ThermalPolicy = structure {

  // ==========================================================================
  // §1's enumeration.
  //
  // INTENT_UNSET is 0 -- the .bss zero-init state, mapped to
  // THERMAL_POLICY_INTENT_BAD by every reader. NONE is a non-zero value
  // deliberately so a level whose meaning was never installed can be
  // distinguished from one whose meaning is NONE by policy.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let THERMAL_POLICY_INTENT_UNSET    : u64 = 0
  pub let THERMAL_POLICY_INTENT_NONE     : u64 = 1
  pub let THERMAL_POLICY_INTENT_ACTIVE   : u64 = 2
  pub let THERMAL_POLICY_INTENT_CRITICAL : u64 = 3
  pub let THERMAL_POLICY_INTENT_MAX      : u64 = 3

  // §2's ladder. Matches `kind_thermal_zone.pdx`'s THERMAL_LVL_*, but the
  // constants are re-declared here rather than imported so the two enums
  // can drift independently -- adding a level to the kind's ladder must
  // then also be an install in the map, and the build fails on any level
  // this table would not answer for.
  pub let THERMAL_POLICY_LVL_MAX     : u64 = 5   // = THERMAL_LVL_CRITICAL
  pub let THERMAL_POLICY_MAP_ENTRIES : u64 = 8   // 6 used, 2 spare

  // ==========================================================================
  // §5's failure taxonomy -- 0xFFFFFE20..0xFFFFFE2A.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let THERMAL_POLICY_OK          : u64 = 0
  pub let THERMAL_POLICY_BAD_LEVEL   : u64 = 0xFFFFFE2A
  pub let THERMAL_POLICY_BAD_INTENT  : u64 = 0xFFFFFE29
  pub let THERMAL_POLICY_MAP_ALREADY : u64 = 0xFFFFFE28

  // The sentinel every reader returns for a value it cannot resolve. Not
  // in the 0xFFFFFExx band because it is a POSITIONAL answer (a level's
  // intent) rather than a REFUSAL of an operation. Same shape as
  // THERMAL_DECODE_BAD in kind_thermal_zone.pdx.
  pub let THERMAL_POLICY_INTENT_BAD  : u64 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF

  // ==========================================================================
  // The table. THE ONLY PLACE IN THE KERNEL where a level-to-intent
  // meaning lives. `tools/build.sh` confines relocations against this
  // symbol to this object, so the mint gate below is the only path to a
  // write -- the same discipline `_thermal_zone_table` holds, for the
  // same reason.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let mut _thermal_policy_map : [u64; 8] = uninit @align(64)

  // ==========================================================================
  // thermal_policy_map_reset() -> ()
  //
  // Clear every level to UNSET. Boot and teardown only. NOT a violation
  // of §2's monotonicity: what monotonicity forbids is a level acquiring
  // a second meaning while the map is in service, and this runs when no
  // consumer can be reading. Bounded by an immediate compare, 8
  // iterations. Leaf. Labels prefixed tp_rs_ per reserved-label
  // discipline.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let thermal_policy_map_reset : () -> () !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M2-003 (#1096): return every entry of _thermal_policy_map to UNSET (0). Called by the witness on entry and on both exits; a fixture whose failure path left an installed meaning would make every later witness observe a map some earlier one wrote. Loop bound is an immediate 8, not a `sizeof(_thermal_policy_map) / 8`, because the two shapes fail differently under a table resize -- an immediate refuses to build, a computed bound silently sweeps whatever the new size is -- and this file's size is a review artefact. Leaf, no nested calls. Labels prefixed tp_rs_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        lea r10, [rip + _thermal_policy_map];
        xor rax, rax;
        xor rcx, rcx;
      tp_rs_loop:
        mov [r10 + rcx * 8], rax;
        add rcx, 1;
        cmp rcx, 8;
        jb  tp_rs_loop;
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  // ==========================================================================
  // thermal_policy_map_install(level, intent) -> THERMAL_POLICY_OK
  //                                            | THERMAL_POLICY_BAD_LEVEL
  //                                            | THERMAL_POLICY_BAD_INTENT
  //                                            | THERMAL_POLICY_MAP_ALREADY
  //
  // §2's install. Gates, in order:
  //
  //   (1) level in 0..THERMAL_POLICY_LVL_MAX -> BAD_LEVEL otherwise.
  //   (2) intent in 1..THERMAL_POLICY_INTENT_MAX -> BAD_INTENT otherwise.
  //       UNSET (0) as a stored value would be an entry that later reads
  //       as bad, and the install path is where an unsettable meaning
  //       must be refused. There is no "install NONE" convenience: NONE
  //       is a value the caller must ask for by name.
  //   (3) MONOTONICITY -- if the entry already holds a non-UNSET intent,
  //       a request naming the SAME intent returns OK (idempotent) and a
  //       request naming a DIFFERENT one returns MAP_ALREADY. Total re-
  //       tuning is `_reset` followed by fresh installs; that is the
  //       shape reviewable in a single call site.
  //
  // Leaf. Labels prefixed tp_mi_ per reserved-label discipline.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let thermal_policy_map_install : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (level: u64, intent: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M2-003 (#1096): install one level's cooling intent. Gate (1): level > THERMAL_POLICY_LVL_MAX -> BAD_LEVEL. Level 5 is CRITICAL and is a legitimate installation target for INTENT_CRITICAL; refusing at MAX+1 rather than at MAX is what lets the CRITICAL level acquire a meaning at all, and a bug that flipped the compare to jae would leave the most important level un-installable while every other test still passed. Gate (2): intent == 0 or intent > THERMAL_POLICY_INTENT_MAX -> BAD_INTENT. Zero is a stored UNSET; storing it would produce an entry every reader answers BAD for, which is a way to un-install a level without the reset that lets a caller see it. Refusing zero here is the sibling of refusing byte-0 in ec_event_map_set. Gate (3): the monotonicity gate. Read the current value: 0 (UNSET) -> proceed to store; equal to `intent` -> return OK without a store (a caller that installs the map twice has achieved what it asked for); different -> MAP_ALREADY. THIS IS THE MOST LOAD-BEARING REFUSAL IN THE FILE. A NOMINAL that meant NONE at boot and ACTIVE an hour later reinterprets the same reading with no symptom, exactly the shape the ec_event_map_set justification writes at length; the two refusals must fail in the same way for the same reason. Offset is level * 8, a constant shift after the bounds check. Leaf, no nested calls. Labels prefixed tp_mi_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        mov r11, 5;                      // THERMAL_POLICY_LVL_MAX
        cmp rdi, r11;
        ja  tp_mi_bad_level;

        cmp rsi, 0;
        je  tp_mi_bad_intent;
        mov r11, 3;                      // THERMAL_POLICY_INTENT_MAX
        cmp rsi, r11;
        ja  tp_mi_bad_intent;

        mov r8, rdi;
        shl r8, 3;
        lea r9, [rip + _thermal_policy_map];
        add r8, r9;
        mov rax, [r8 + 0];
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  tp_mi_store;                 // UNSET: fill it
        cmp rax, rsi;
        je  tp_mi_same;                  // same intent: idempotent OK
        jmp tp_mi_already;

      tp_mi_store:
        mov [r8 + 0], rsi;
      tp_mi_same:
        xor rax, rax;                    // THERMAL_POLICY_OK
        ret;

      tp_mi_bad_level:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFE2A;             // THERMAL_POLICY_BAD_LEVEL
        ret;
      tp_mi_bad_intent:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFE29;             // THERMAL_POLICY_BAD_INTENT
        ret;
      tp_mi_already:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFE28;             // THERMAL_POLICY_MAP_ALREADY
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  // ==========================================================================
  // thermal_policy_intent_of_level(level) -> intent | THERMAL_POLICY_INTENT_BAD
  //
  // §2's lookup. Pure and total over the level enumeration; refuses OOB
  // and refuses UNSET. Level 0..5 is a KIND_THERMAL_ZONE constant, not a
  // temperature -- see the ladder in `kind_thermal_zone.pdx` §4.
  //
  // ARITY ONE. A second parameter would let a caller supply the intent
  // and receive it back -- the shape of a lookup that cannot say anything
  // was not there. `tools/build.sh` pins the signature.
  //
  // Leaf. Labels prefixed tp_il_ per reserved-label discipline.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let thermal_policy_intent_of_level : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (level: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M2-003 (#1096): the intent for a level, or THERMAL_POLICY_INTENT_BAD. RETURNS THE SENTINEL FOR BOTH OOB AND UNSET, and the caller is expected to distinguish those two cases by knowing which levels it installed. That is the same shape thermal_zone_level_of_slot uses (THERMAL_DECODE_BAD for both a dead row and an OOB slot) and for the same reason: an intent answer is a value in the intent enum, and a REFUSAL is a value NOT in the enum -- one code covers every non-answer, and a caller that acts on a non-answer as if it were an intent has a bug distinguishable from every real reading. ARITY ONE, PINNED. Offset is level * 8, a constant shift after the bounds check. Leaf, no nested calls. Labels prefixed tp_il_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        mov r11, 5;                      // THERMAL_POLICY_LVL_MAX
        cmp rdi, r11;
        ja  tp_il_bad;

        mov r8, rdi;
        shl r8, 3;
        lea r9, [rip + _thermal_policy_map];
        add r8, r9;
        mov rax, [r8 + 0];
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  tp_il_bad;                   // UNSET
        ret;

      tp_il_bad:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;     // THERMAL_POLICY_INTENT_BAD
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  // ==========================================================================
  // thermal_policy_intent_of_slot(slot) -> intent | THERMAL_POLICY_INTENT_BAD
  //
  // §3's composed read. First consult the critical latch; if latched,
  // the intent is CRITICAL regardless of the current level. Otherwise
  // read the level and consult the table.
  //
  // The LATCH-FIRST ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING and stated at length in §3 --
  // a consumer that read the level and then noticed the latch would
  // silently report NONE for a still-dangerous zone whose temperature
  // had dipped below _CRT.
  //
  // ARITY ONE. A shutdown predicate that took a level argument would be
  // a shutdown predicate a caller could talk out of firing; the mirror
  // of `thermal_zone_crt_latched_of_slot`'s arity discipline. Pinned by
  // `tools/build.sh`.
  //
  // Labels prefixed tp_is_ per reserved-label discipline.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let thermal_policy_intent_of_slot : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{cap} =
    fn (slot: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {cap},
      justification: "R31.M2-003 (#1096): the intent this zone's current state warrants. Two nested reads, and the ORDER is the substance of the function. Read (a): thermal_zone_crt_latched_of_slot. If 1, return INTENT_CRITICAL immediately -- the latch is the row's memory that the two-agreeing-reads gate fired, and re-deriving the decision from the current level would erase that memory the moment temperature dipped under _CRT (see kind's §3). If the answer is not 0 either -- THERMAL_DECODE_BAD for a slot that does not hold a zone -- propagate THERMAL_POLICY_INTENT_BAD, because a policy answer for a non-zone slot is a lie a caller would act on. Read (b): thermal_zone_level_of_slot. Same THERMAL_DECODE_BAD propagation. Otherwise consult thermal_policy_intent_of_level, whose own answer is either a valid intent or THERMAL_POLICY_INTENT_BAD for OOB / UNSET. THREE PUSHES (rbx for the slot, r12/r13 for scratch across the nested calls) leave rsp%16 == 0 at every call site. Labels prefixed tp_is_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;
        mov rbx, rdi;                    // slot

        // (a) latch first.
        mov rdi, rbx;
        call thermal_zone_crt_latched_of_slot;
        mov r11, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        cmp rax, r11;
        je  tp_is_bad;                   // dead slot
        cmp rax, 1;
        je  tp_is_critical;              // latched

        // (b) level, then table.
        mov rdi, rbx;
        call thermal_zone_level_of_slot;
        mov r11, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        cmp rax, r11;
        je  tp_is_bad;                   // dead slot

        mov rdi, rax;
        call thermal_policy_intent_of_level;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret;

      tp_is_critical:
        mov rax, 3;                      // THERMAL_POLICY_INTENT_CRITICAL
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret;

      tp_is_bad:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;     // THERMAL_POLICY_INTENT_BAD
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }
}
